@Author CameronPinchin

Personal project, generally for practicing C. 
Started because I wanted to try to restart SSHD on my Pi without plugging in a keyboard.
 - Couldn't connect via ssh to Pi.
 - Swiftly became aware that restarting sshd.service isn't going to fix the issue.
 - Continued anyways because its a good project.

This is an interface for systemctl commands, where the server is hosted on the RPi.
The server receives datagrams from clients, that contain a service and an action. 
  i.e., "./<client_binary> <action> <service>"

The server then tries to run the corresponding command and let the client know if it succeeded or failed.

POSIX-compliant, but not portable. The commands rely on the systemctl to interface with the systemd service manager, which is Linux-specific. Otherwise, everything is POSIX-compliant.

To enable this daemon to be launched at boot-time, do the following:

1. Make any desired modifications to the rpi5daemon.service file.

2. Save the file to "/etc/systemd/system/server_arm64.service"
  # mv ${PROJECT_DIRECTORY}/rpi5daemon.service /etc/systemd/system/server_arm64

3. Move the server_arm64 binary to your "/usr/bin" path.
  # mv ${PROJECT_DIRECTORY}/rpi5-daemon-server/bin/server_arm64 /usr/bin

4. Reload the daemon:
  # systemctl daemon-reload

5. Enable the service (tells systemd to start this service on startup)
  # systemctl enable server_arm64

6. Start the service.
  # systemctl start server_arm64

